<title>OMAN: Gulf Air says facing debts of $2 bln.</title>
<headline>Gulf Air says facing debts of $2 bln.</headline>
<dateline>MUSCAT 1996-11-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Gulf Air's president said late on Wednesday the carrier was facing debts of $2 billion, but added the troubled airline had managed to post a $13 million profit during the summer season.</p>
<p>&quot;We made a five million Bahraini dinar ($13.25 million) profit from June to October this year,&quot; Sheikh Ahmad bin Seif al-Nahayan told reporters after a board of directors meeting in the Omani capital Muscat.</p>
<p>&quot;During the same period last year, the company suffered a loss of 4.7 million Bahraini dinars,&quot; he added.</p>
<p>Bahrain-based Gulf Air -- which is jointly owned by the governments of Bahrain, Qatar, Oman and Abu Dhabi -- made a $159 million loss in 1995.</p>
<p>($1=0.377 dinars)</p>
</text>
